The GIL lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time, so CPU‑bound threads contend for it and see little speedup, while I/O‑bound threads often release the GIL during system calls and can run concurrently. In practice, a CPU‑heavy loop in multiple threads runs almost as fast as a single thread, whereas many network or file reads scale with thread count.  

```python
import threading, time, urllib.request

def cpu_task():
    x = 0
    for _ in range(10_000_000):
        x += 1

def io_task():
    urllib.request.urlopen('https://httpbin.org/delay/1').read()

def run(fn, n):
    start = time.time()
    threads = [threading.Thread(target=fn) for _ in range(n)]
    for t in threads: t.start()
    for t in threads: t.join()
    print(f'{fn.__name__} x{n}: {time.time()-start:.2f}s')

run(cpu_task, 4)   # little improvement over 1 thread
run(io_task, 4)    # roughly 1 s instead of 4 s
```

