The Circuit Breaker pattern prevents a failing service from repeatedly trying to execute a call that is likely to fail, protecting the system from cascading failures. It tracks failures and, after a threshold, opens the circuit so subsequent calls return immediately or fallback logic, then periodically tries to close it once the service appears healthy.  

In a Spring Boot microservice you can use Resilience4j:

```java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "myService", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
public String callRemote() {
    return restTemplate.getForObject("http://remote/api", String.class);
}

private String fallback(Throwable t) {
    return "Service unavailable – fallback response";
}
```

This opens the circuit after a set number of failures, then retries after a cooldown period.

